  "question": {
    "text": "A natural number has prime factorization given by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></msup><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>y</mi></msup><msup><mn>5</mn><mi>z</mi></msup></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>z</mi></math> are such that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>z</mi></math>. Then the number of odd divisors of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi></math>, including <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>, is:<gwmw style=\"display:none;\"></gwmw>",
    "image": null
  },
